1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:04,800
Our great buildings proclaim our ability to shape our world,

2
00:00:05,160 --> 00:00:07,840
but sometimes the unexpected brings tragedy.

3
00:00:08,780 --> 00:00:13,180
And now, terrorists attack our buildings as symbols of a culture they despise.

4
00:00:13,560 --> 00:00:15,960
Are the buildings we live and work in safe,

5
00:00:16,080 --> 00:00:18,840
or do they help bring about destruction and death?

6
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:36,540
Buildings look like static objects, but they are really machines.

7
00:00:36,740 --> 00:00:37,800
They have moving parts.

8
00:00:38,320 --> 00:00:40,200
They have heating and ventilation equipment.

9
00:00:40,660 --> 00:00:41,940
They have elevators.

10
00:00:42,480 --> 00:00:43,660
They have stairways.

11
00:00:44,280 --> 00:00:46,580
And they have lots of energy.

12
00:00:47,920 --> 00:00:53,020
And that energy, when released, has the real potential to invite disaster.

13
00:00:53,021 --> 00:01:05,140
Since the dawn of civilization, humankind has sought to proclaim its unique intelligence and mastery of its environment by creating buildings.

14
00:01:06,100 --> 00:01:12,680
The human mind could transform wood and stone into structures that provided more than shelter from the elements.

15
00:01:13,500 --> 00:01:19,320
Humans hoped to create buildings that would represent them to the ages and become symbols of their achievements.

16
00:01:19,321 --> 00:01:26,320
But often these symbols lacked a durability that matched the vision that created them.

17
00:01:26,321 --> 00:01:33,320
Since the first mud bricks were stacked to form a wall, humans have struggled to erect buildings that would stay up.

18
00:01:33,321 --> 00:01:41,320
The seven wonders of the ancient world were created to represent the skill and power of the societies that built them.

19
00:01:41,321 --> 00:01:42,607
that built them.

20
00:01:42,608 --> 00:01:48,320
But all these ancient wonders have fallen to vandals or natural forces,

21
00:01:48,660 --> 00:01:54,160
all save one.

22
00:01:54,161 --> 00:01:59,580
The pyramids at Giza were constructed over 4,000 years ago to proclaim the greatness

23
00:01:59,581 --> 00:02:01,554
of three Old Kingdom pharaohs.

24
00:02:01,555 --> 00:02:05,700
While the hanging gardens of Babylon and the colossus of Rhodes

25
00:02:05,701 --> 00:02:08,500
have become dust, the pyramids remain.

26
00:02:09,420 --> 00:02:12,240
Their architect wisely placed them on solid bedrock.

27
00:02:13,200 --> 00:02:18,360
The Pyramid of Khufu alone contains 6.5 million tons of stone

28
00:02:18,361 --> 00:02:21,180
that still stand in almost perfect alignment.

29
00:02:22,120 --> 00:02:25,360
But even Egypt's master builders made mistakes.

30
00:02:26,220 --> 00:02:29,060
If you look at the Pyramid of Khufu, the Great Pyramid,

31
00:02:29,180 --> 00:02:30,520
the one that we're all familiar with,

32
00:02:30,521 --> 00:02:36,280
You think nothing could ever bring down that man-made mountain.

33
00:02:36,900 --> 00:02:42,420
But not too far from that great pyramid is a lesson called the Pyramid of Midom.

34
00:02:43,100 --> 00:02:47,120
And that pyramid did encounter the unexpected.

35
00:02:48,000 --> 00:02:55,060
The architects of Midom forgot that the above-ground mass was meaningless unless well-supported below ground.

36
00:02:55,620 --> 00:02:58,120
The pyramid at Midom no longer looks like a pyramid.

37
00:02:58,121 --> 00:03:06,380
It was so poorly built that it could not support the casing stones that once lined its surface and now lie crumbled at its base.

38
00:03:06,860 --> 00:03:09,860
All that remains is the pyramid's inner core.

39
00:03:10,700 --> 00:03:14,580
They kept pushing the envelope as time went on.

40
00:03:15,020 --> 00:03:21,000
And when Midem was built, it was built on sand, and sands shift over time.

41
00:03:21,580 --> 00:03:27,080
So as the sands began to move, these stones began to shift away from the center.

42
00:03:27,081 --> 00:03:28,680
and many of them fell over.

43
00:03:32,240 --> 00:03:35,880
Not even the passage of centuries cured human forgetfulness

44
00:03:35,881 --> 00:03:37,800
about the importance of foundations.

45
00:03:39,560 --> 00:03:41,440
Aesthetics in a building are meaningless

46
00:03:41,441 --> 00:03:43,900
unless they are combined with sound engineering.

47
00:03:46,020 --> 00:03:49,740
In the 12th century, towers in Italian towns and cities

48
00:03:49,741 --> 00:03:53,120
expressed a civic pride and demonstrated to their world

49
00:03:53,121 --> 00:03:54,860
the town's wealth and accomplishment.

50
00:03:54,861 --> 00:04:03,140
Each town's efforts grew more and more elaborate as they built higher and higher in a race to create the most impressive towers.

51
00:04:05,780 --> 00:04:14,680
In 1174, the leaders of the Italian city of Pisa decided to add to the beauty of their town by building a bell tower of their own.

52
00:04:15,540 --> 00:04:20,140
The fact is that people didn't know that much about foundation construction,

53
00:04:20,800 --> 00:04:25,500
and so the Pisa Tower was built on a clay type of foundation

54
00:04:25,501 --> 00:04:28,320
that tends to compress over time because it's near the water,

55
00:04:28,680 --> 00:04:32,920
so that when the water is squeezed out of the clay, it tends to sink.

56
00:04:33,340 --> 00:04:36,980
And in that particular case, it tended to sink unequally.

57
00:04:38,120 --> 00:04:40,780
Almost immediately, the tower began to tilt,

58
00:04:40,781 --> 00:04:45,140
and as the centuries have passed, it has leaned 15 feet out of alignment.

59
00:04:45,840 --> 00:04:47,380
But it could have been worse.

60
00:04:49,400 --> 00:04:53,500
Pisa is a tower that stands due to circumstance,

61
00:04:53,800 --> 00:04:59,940
and the circumstance was the builders at Pisa could not proceed on the original construction schedule.

62
00:05:00,440 --> 00:05:02,700
They had to hold up twice.

63
00:05:03,300 --> 00:05:06,680
In fact, the whole construction of the tower took over 200 years.

64
00:05:06,681 --> 00:05:17,260
The reason that was fortuitous was it gave the weak ground time to find an equilibrium underneath that tower of Pisa.

65
00:05:17,560 --> 00:05:22,380
Had they built on a fast track, like we like to build our buildings today,

66
00:05:22,760 --> 00:05:27,520
experts think the Leaning Tower of Pisa would not be a tower at all.

67
00:05:27,580 --> 00:05:29,400
It would be rubble on the ground.

68
00:05:30,400 --> 00:05:33,620
Thousands come every year to see the famous Leaning Tower.

69
00:05:33,621 --> 00:05:41,620
The tilt has been virtually stopped by adding a network of cables to counteract the pull of gravity and pull the tower back toward vertical.

70
00:05:42,620 --> 00:05:49,620
After over 800 years, Pisa's beautiful tower has managed to survive its builders' mistakes.

71
00:05:54,620 --> 00:05:59,620
But not all defects in the construction of a building show up as quickly as they did at Pisa.

72
00:05:59,621 --> 00:06:00,321
at Pisa.

73
00:06:00,322 --> 00:06:05,500
In another Renaissance bell tower, the structural flaws remained hidden for centuries.

74
00:06:09,280 --> 00:06:14,440
No one who visits Venice is likely to forget the graceful campanile that soars over the

75
00:06:14,441 --> 00:06:15,973
Piazza of San Marco.

76
00:06:15,974 --> 00:06:20,880
But few visitors here realize the building that so enchants them

77
00:06:20,881 --> 00:06:22,063
is not original.

78
00:06:22,064 --> 00:06:27,240
Poor construction or a shift in its foundation almost robbed Venice of

79
00:06:27,241 --> 00:06:34,634
its lovely tower. In 1902, the original bell tower, over 800 years old, fell in.

80
00:06:34,635 --> 00:06:36,020
The good story,

81
00:06:36,180 --> 00:06:40,226
there were no casualties. The only loss was the caretaker's cat.

82
00:06:40,227 --> 00:06:41,680
Some people, especially

83
00:06:41,681 --> 00:06:47,074
romantic Italians, attributed to old age. It simply got too old and it came down.

84
00:06:47,075 --> 00:06:47,775
I think

85
00:06:47,776 --> 00:06:50,201
it's more likely that it was built in Venice.

86
00:06:50,202 --> 00:06:53,040
Venice, as we know, is built on islands that are

87
00:06:53,041 --> 00:06:59,400
actually sinking over time and it's very possible that the foundation again began to fail.

88
00:07:00,560 --> 00:07:05,460
Some have noted that the original structure may have been weakened during remodeling.

89
00:07:06,480 --> 00:07:11,900
Others have cited lightning strikes and still others feel centuries of clanging bells weakened

90
00:07:11,901 --> 00:07:13,796
the tower until it collapsed.

91
00:07:13,797 --> 00:07:17,720
But for Venetians it is only important that original or not,

92
00:07:17,900 --> 00:07:21,820
the wonderful tower remains as the most famous landmark of their city.

93
00:07:21,821 --> 00:07:29,760
With the passage of time, eventually many of us will never know whether it was original or whether it was a recreation.

94
00:07:30,160 --> 00:07:33,460
And it really won't matter because buildings are not like people.

95
00:07:33,880 --> 00:07:36,540
They can have a second life. They can have a third life.

96
00:07:39,180 --> 00:07:46,460
Renaissance bell towers were not the only structures where sound building techniques were threatened by the urge to impress one's neighbors.

97
00:07:47,080 --> 00:07:50,260
In medieval Europe, some local bishops or cardinals

98
00:07:50,261 --> 00:07:54,240
set out to build churches on a scale that was more grand and impressive

99
00:07:54,241 --> 00:07:56,120
than those built by their colleagues.

100
00:07:57,740 --> 00:08:01,740
In 1247, the bishop of a small town in France

101
00:08:01,741 --> 00:08:04,500
set out to build the grandest of them all.

102
00:08:05,660 --> 00:08:08,380
The bishop near Beauvais, France,

103
00:08:08,460 --> 00:08:11,920
got into a disagreement with the king, with the royalty of France.

104
00:08:11,921 --> 00:08:21,060
And his expression of competing, of trying to exceed the power of the crown,

105
00:08:21,220 --> 00:08:25,220
was to put up the grandest of the Gothic cathedrals.

106
00:08:28,600 --> 00:08:32,860
The vaulted ceiling that soared 150 feet over the choir

107
00:08:32,861 --> 00:08:38,380
hinted at a finished church that would rival St. Peter's in Rome in style and scale.

108
00:08:38,381 --> 00:08:44,120
But the support for the great weight of the vaults was poorly conceived and executed.

109
00:08:44,960 --> 00:08:48,540
In November 1284, the enormous structure collapsed.

110
00:08:50,900 --> 00:08:57,500
In 1564, another attempt was made to erect the complete cathedral, but this also collapsed.

111
00:08:59,100 --> 00:09:04,740
Over the centuries, the great church has been partially rebuilt with added structural support.

112
00:09:04,741 --> 00:09:08,280
But the great nave of the cathedral was never finished.

113
00:09:08,380 --> 00:09:15,640
What remains in Beauvais is a testament to determination, as well as an example of flawed architectural planning.

114
00:09:16,420 --> 00:09:25,820
The understanding of the science behind buildings really did not occur until the, let's say, late 17th, early 18th centuries.

115
00:09:26,360 --> 00:09:32,280
And since then, and it's been evolving since then, now we have science behind us.

116
00:09:32,340 --> 00:09:33,880
We are able to make calculations.

117
00:09:34,580 --> 00:09:37,620
And because of computers, we can make very elaborate calculations.

118
00:09:37,621 --> 00:09:42,540
That's why our buildings today are so much lighter than they were even 100 years ago.

119
00:09:44,160 --> 00:09:48,340
But technological advances and contemporary scientific calculation

120
00:09:48,341 --> 00:09:53,040
cannot make up for what causes so many failures in the buildings we create.

121
00:09:53,900 --> 00:09:59,680
Even in modern times, it is often simple human error that continues to invite disaster.

122
00:10:02,880 --> 00:10:06,620
Elisha Otis demonstrated his safety elevator to crowds in 1854.

123
00:10:07,620 --> 00:10:11,800
by standing in a suspended elevator and having the hoist rope cut with an axe.

124
00:10:12,480 --> 00:10:15,040
Inviting Disaster will continue on Modern Marvels.

125
00:10:17,760 --> 00:10:22,740
Those of us who go into buildings hope that engineers really know what they're doing,

126
00:10:22,741 --> 00:10:27,660
that they meet the highest standards of foresight and thoroughness.

127
00:10:27,900 --> 00:10:34,880
They put that extra measure of devotion in, not just to the building, but to the people who will use it.

128
00:10:37,620 --> 00:10:42,180
This tower is seen the world over as a graceful expression of design.

129
00:10:42,960 --> 00:10:51,860
Its height and the complexity of its interlaced wrought iron components required an engineer of imagination and, equally as important, obsession with detail.

130
00:10:52,520 --> 00:10:54,460
Gustav Eiffel was such a talent.

131
00:10:56,220 --> 00:11:05,520
Such a thorough engineer with his thousands of drawings that out of thousands of structural members that went into the tower, they all fit.

132
00:11:05,521 --> 00:11:14,680
To me, the Eiffel Tower stands in sharp contrast to a project such as the Hartford Civic Center Coliseum.

133
00:11:16,180 --> 00:11:20,740
In 1970, the city of Hartford, Connecticut, decided to build a civic center.

134
00:11:21,580 --> 00:11:28,160
Architects and engineers came up with a unique roof design of linked pyramids that would cover almost two and a half acres.

135
00:11:28,161 --> 00:11:37,160
But even before the unique roof was lifted into place, observers began to notice that, unlike the Eiffel Tower, the pieces in this building didn't fit.

136
00:11:37,161 --> 00:11:47,160
Contractors complained that they were not able to assemble the structure very easily because the bolts would not fit through the holes.

137
00:11:47,161 --> 00:11:57,160
Passers-by, not casual passers-by, but architects or engineers noticed that the roof seemed to sag under its own load quite a bit.

138
00:11:57,161 --> 00:12:00,960
quite a bit, and yet nobody listened.

139
00:12:00,961 --> 00:12:04,660
We should know now that's a problem.

140
00:12:04,661 --> 00:12:10,420
If bolt holes don't line up, it's time to take a break.

141
00:12:10,421 --> 00:12:15,400
Bring in the experts and see whether or not that's a severe problem.

142
00:12:15,401 --> 00:12:17,480
It was a severe problem.

143
00:12:17,481 --> 00:12:23,860
On the night of January 18, 1978, as fans watched a basketball game in the Civic Center,

144
00:12:23,861 --> 00:12:26,660
a heavy snow fell outside.

145
00:12:26,661 --> 00:12:32,660
Later that night, the snow-covered 1,400-ton roof collapsed in a mighty roar.

146
00:12:32,661 --> 00:12:40,520
Had it failed six hours earlier, it would have fallen on 5,000 people.

147
00:12:40,521 --> 00:12:44,840
The pyramid grid of the roof on the Hartford Civic Center bent under stress in ways never

148
00:12:44,841 --> 00:12:47,560
considered in the design.

149
00:12:47,561 --> 00:12:52,960
But the most disturbing factor was that so many warnings had been given and ignored.

150
00:12:52,961 --> 00:12:57,740
The Civic Center had fallen because of the failure of hundreds of structural parts, but

151
00:12:57,741 --> 00:13:03,360
some disasters offer few hints of their approach and come about because of only one piece of

152
00:13:03,361 --> 00:13:05,560
a huge structure.

153
00:13:05,561 --> 00:13:14,800
In 1928, the Silver Bridge was completed, connecting the town of Point Pleasant, West

154
00:13:14,801 --> 00:13:20,680
Virginia with Gallipolis, Ohio across the Ohio River.

155
00:13:20,681 --> 00:13:25,740
As it opened for traffic, the bridge was considered a marvel of engineering.

156
00:13:25,741 --> 00:13:30,840
Instead of being suspended by cables, the span depended on 50-foot rods that were joined

157
00:13:30,841 --> 00:13:36,500
by flat steel plates held together with large metal pins.

158
00:13:36,501 --> 00:13:41,160
It was as if the bridge was held up by massive bicycle chains.

159
00:13:41,161 --> 00:13:47,120
On December 15th, 1967, the bridge was crowded with rush hour cars.

160
00:13:47,121 --> 00:13:54,120
At 5.15 in the afternoon, the 1,400-foot bridge that had been hailed as a marble collapsed.

161
00:13:54,121 --> 00:13:57,880
31 cars fell into the Ohio River.

162
00:13:57,881 --> 00:14:03,200
Research would show that the failure of only one plate in only one of the chain's links

163
00:14:03,201 --> 00:14:07,500
caused 46 people to lose their lives.

164
00:14:07,501 --> 00:14:09,700
But there was good to come out of this tragedy.

165
00:14:09,701 --> 00:14:14,160
The Silver Bridge caused President Lyndon Johnson to order an inquiry into bridge safety throughout

166
00:14:14,161 --> 00:14:15,440
the nation.

167
00:14:15,441 --> 00:14:22,680
More than 7,000 bridges have been inspected and repaired in a process that is still underway.

168
00:14:22,681 --> 00:14:30,240
No matter how big the structure, the smallest of parts can cause terrible disasters.

169
00:14:30,241 --> 00:14:34,260
Friday July 17th 1981.

170
00:14:34,261 --> 00:14:40,180
1,600 people gathered for a dance in the atrium of the Hyatt Regency Hotel in Kansas City.

171
00:14:40,181 --> 00:14:44,944
None of them guessed they would be part of the worst structural disaster in U.S.

172
00:14:44,945 --> 00:14:45,645
history.

173
00:14:45,646 --> 00:14:49,720
Three suspended walkways crossed the 120-foot wide atrium.

174
00:14:49,721 --> 00:14:54,340
The original plans called for the walkways to hang from individual rods running from

175
00:14:54,341 --> 00:14:56,100
floor to ceiling.

176
00:14:56,101 --> 00:15:01,560
Instead of that, the contractor substituted separate rods between the roof and the top

177
00:15:01,561 --> 00:15:04,300
level and the next level down.

178
00:15:04,301 --> 00:15:10,180
And the designer or one of his people, unfortunately, one of his younger engineers approved the

179
00:15:10,181 --> 00:15:15,940
change it was an improper change the design changes were signed as reviewed

180
00:15:15,941 --> 00:15:20,140
and approved by the supervising engineers but the plans went into

181
00:15:20,141 --> 00:15:26,920
construction untested substituting two runs for one put more weight on each of

182
00:15:26,921 --> 00:15:31,000
the transverse beams holding the fourth floor walkway as the crowd packed onto

183
00:15:31,001 --> 00:15:36,700
the walkways they danced to the music in a moment the party became a nightmare

184
00:15:36,701 --> 00:15:40,920
Two of the steel-framed walkways crashed down on the crowd below.

185
00:15:41,660 --> 00:15:46,480
114 people died, and 200 were injured, many maimed for life.

186
00:15:47,680 --> 00:15:50,220
Designers had failed to plan for the unexpected.

187
00:15:50,700 --> 00:15:54,400
They had added no redundancy to the structure of the Hyatt Regency walkways.

188
00:15:55,100 --> 00:15:58,700
When faced with a crisis, the walkways could not handle the unexpected.

189
00:15:59,980 --> 00:16:03,460
What they should do is think skeptically,

190
00:16:03,461 --> 00:16:08,120
this is a fairly large structure that can hold a lot of people,

191
00:16:09,340 --> 00:16:11,800
is my design able to take it?

192
00:16:12,040 --> 00:16:16,780
Can it take anything that time can throw at it?

193
00:16:17,860 --> 00:16:21,360
Unfortunately, time can throw a great many things at buildings,

194
00:16:21,840 --> 00:16:26,460
especially when our buildings grow to become symbols of the society that builds them.

195
00:16:29,020 --> 00:16:33,380
The Pont de Gare Bridge near Nîmes, France, is 900 feet long.

196
00:16:33,460 --> 00:16:38,460
and 160 feet high and has stood almost 2,000 years.

197
00:16:38,461 --> 00:16:43,460
Inviting disaster will continue on Modern Marvels.

198
00:16:43,461 --> 00:16:45,960
We think of a big building as holding certain things.

199
00:16:45,961 --> 00:16:51,060
It holds people, it holds furniture, lots of paperwork.

200
00:16:51,061 --> 00:16:53,660
But in fact, a tall building holds something else,

201
00:16:53,661 --> 00:16:57,260
a tremendous amount of energy that is really captured.

202
00:16:57,261 --> 00:17:01,060
It is caged inside that structure from the early days

203
00:17:01,061 --> 00:17:08,320
its construction when cranes used lots of power to raise thousands of tons high into the air.

204
00:17:08,500 --> 00:17:11,880
And that energy was just waiting for a trigger to be released.

205
00:17:16,740 --> 00:17:22,680
Throughout history, buildings had been limited by how high one stone could be stacked on top of

206
00:17:22,681 --> 00:17:23,381
another.

207
00:17:23,382 --> 00:17:27,560
The endurance of the buildings was dependent only on natural materials to make

208
00:17:27,561 --> 00:17:30,045
them strong and prevent their collapse.

209
00:17:30,046 --> 00:17:33,040
But by the end of the 19th century, human skill

210
00:17:33,041 --> 00:17:38,440
and imagination were ready to create a whole new class of buildings that grew up as well

211
00:17:38,441 --> 00:17:39,141
as out.

212
00:17:39,142 --> 00:17:44,280
It was a change that would require new margins of safety and even greater concern

213
00:17:44,281 --> 00:17:45,610
for the unexpected.

214
00:17:45,611 --> 00:17:50,160
These buildings would find strength and stability not in rock and

215
00:17:50,161 --> 00:17:56,080
brick and mortar, but in steel.

216
00:17:56,081 --> 00:18:00,260
The invention of the Bessemer process made it possible to create steel that was not only

217
00:18:00,261 --> 00:18:05,940
stronger than anything that had preceded it, but could be produced cheaply in a diversity

218
00:18:05,941 --> 00:18:08,900
of lengths and shapes.

219
00:18:08,901 --> 00:18:14,020
But it also took another invention by a mechanic from Yonkers, New York, to at last allow buildings

220
00:18:14,021 --> 00:18:15,800
to grow skyward.

221
00:18:15,801 --> 00:18:22,800
In 1854, Elisha Otis had demonstrated safety brakes that could effectively prevent elevator accidents.

222
00:18:22,801 --> 00:18:36,800
With time, the combination of the Otis elevator and improvements in electric motors made possible the high-speed safety elevator that was so smoothly running,

223
00:18:36,801 --> 00:18:43,420
running, people could feel they were in a little room rather than suspended over a hole

224
00:18:43,421 --> 00:18:45,840
hundreds of feet deep.

225
00:18:46,680 --> 00:18:51,820
The first buildings deserving of the name skyscraper appeared in Chicago in the last

226
00:18:51,821 --> 00:18:53,540
decades of the 19th century.

227
00:18:54,160 --> 00:18:59,720
The disastrous Chicago fire of 1871 created a huge demand for new buildings.

228
00:19:00,660 --> 00:19:04,460
In 1884, William LeBaron Jenny met that demand

229
00:19:04,461 --> 00:19:08,640
when he used steel to erect what is arguably the first skyscraper,

230
00:19:09,040 --> 00:19:11,060
the 10-story home insurance building.

231
00:19:12,140 --> 00:19:16,160
In 1891, Louis Sullivan, a student of LeBaron Jenny,

232
00:19:16,360 --> 00:19:18,160
created the sleek Wainwright Building.

233
00:19:18,900 --> 00:19:24,260
And in 1895, Daniel Burnham filled the facade of the 15-story Reliance Building

234
00:19:24,261 --> 00:19:26,960
with large windows never before seen.

235
00:19:27,460 --> 00:19:30,480
Only steel would allow such large openings in the walls.

236
00:19:32,140 --> 00:19:35,840
As the new century began, New York was also growing upward.

237
00:19:36,560 --> 00:19:39,920
In 1903, the intersection of 23rd Street and Broadway

238
00:19:39,921 --> 00:19:43,860
saw the rise of one of America's most popular and photographed structures,

239
00:19:44,260 --> 00:19:46,000
the 20-story Flatiron Building.

240
00:19:46,820 --> 00:19:48,960
City dwellers had never seen anything like it.

241
00:19:48,961 --> 00:19:52,000
The winds that swirled around the daring new structure

242
00:19:52,001 --> 00:19:54,560
kept New Yorkers holding onto their hats.

243
00:19:54,561 --> 00:20:00,320
In 1913, New York grabbed the title of City of Skyscrapers

244
00:20:00,321 --> 00:20:03,280
with the construction of the remarkable Woolworth Building

245
00:20:03,281 --> 00:20:09,700
This masterpiece of Gothic glory soared to the unimaginable height of 792 feet

246
00:20:09,701 --> 00:20:18,980
In 1930, the 70-story Chrysler Building became the tallest structure

247
00:20:18,981 --> 00:20:24,280
But within a year, it gave up the title to the most celebrated skyscraper of all

248
00:20:24,281 --> 00:20:26,480
rising less than a mile away.

249
00:20:28,060 --> 00:20:29,980
Completed in only a year and a half,

250
00:20:30,260 --> 00:20:33,480
the Empire State Building was hailed as the eighth wonder of the world.

251
00:20:34,240 --> 00:20:38,040
It had been only 40 years since Jenny's first 10-story skyscraper.

252
00:20:38,560 --> 00:20:42,980
Now, at 102 stories, the new landmark dwarfed all others.

253
00:20:44,120 --> 00:20:48,040
The skyscraper brought a whole new design challenge for engineers.

254
00:20:48,760 --> 00:20:50,760
In spite of their enormous height and size,

255
00:20:50,761 --> 00:20:54,780
the greatest problem in constructing tall buildings is not their huge weight.

256
00:20:55,440 --> 00:20:58,800
It is the great force of the wind pressing on their sides.

257
00:21:03,020 --> 00:21:05,340
Like all tall buildings of its generation,

258
00:21:05,980 --> 00:21:08,540
the Empire State Building combated the force of the wind

259
00:21:08,541 --> 00:21:11,900
by relying on a forest of steel columns to support it.

260
00:21:12,860 --> 00:21:15,080
Interior spaces were filled with these columns,

261
00:21:15,520 --> 00:21:16,900
restricting the size of offices

262
00:21:16,901 --> 00:21:20,140
and forcing people to work and walk around them.

263
00:21:20,760 --> 00:21:28,340
But the construction of very tall buildings brought other unexpected dangers, never before imagined by engineers.

264
00:21:29,560 --> 00:21:36,960
In 1945, a B-25 bomber, lost in the fog, crashed into the 79th floor of the Empire State Building.

265
00:21:37,700 --> 00:21:42,820
Fourteen people were killed, and a new threat to the world's high buildings had been revealed.

266
00:21:42,821 --> 00:21:52,320
For all its glory, the Empire State Building would mark a pause in American businesses'

267
00:21:52,321 --> 00:21:55,780
fascination with skyscrapers.

268
00:21:55,781 --> 00:22:02,520
It was not until the 1960s that prosperity revived interest in very tall buildings.

269
00:22:02,521 --> 00:22:07,260
New York's Rockefeller Center had been a highly successful innovation for using several tall

270
00:22:07,261 --> 00:22:10,740
buildings as a magnet to attract business.

271
00:22:10,741 --> 00:22:18,120
In the 1960s, the Port Authority of New York and New Jersey set about to use Rockefeller Center as a model to create a similar complex.

272
00:22:18,940 --> 00:22:24,280
A neighborhood of old buildings and small businesses in lower Manhattan would be demolished to make way.

273
00:22:25,500 --> 00:22:34,540
The plan was to build a center in lower Manhattan that would attract, that would be a magnet for world financial trading companies,

274
00:22:35,200 --> 00:22:37,560
and they would call that the World Trade Center.

275
00:22:37,561 --> 00:22:43,560
Architect Minoru Yamasaki was invited to design the World Trade Center.

276
00:22:43,561 --> 00:22:48,560
He responded with a plan for twin towers to be built on a site in Lower Manhattan.

277
00:22:48,561 --> 00:22:51,560
The Port Authority then broadened his assignment.

278
00:22:51,561 --> 00:22:56,560
They asked that the towers become the tallest buildings in the world.

279
00:22:56,561 --> 00:22:59,560
Making them tall was the easy part.

280
00:22:59,561 --> 00:23:04,560
The hard part was that the Port Authority also wanted to do away with a maze of columns

281
00:23:04,561 --> 00:23:09,560
that had marked the interior spaces of virtually all previous tall buildings.

282
00:23:10,560 --> 00:23:14,560
As tenants and owners became more demanding as to what the type of

283
00:23:14,561 --> 00:23:19,560
requirements they wanted to create in their buildings, they were looking for larger column-free spaces.

284
00:23:20,560 --> 00:23:26,560
To solve this challenge, Yamasaki turned to a young structural engineer named Leslie Robertson.

285
00:23:26,561 --> 00:23:36,660
The whole concept of the building required that the office floors be unencumbered by interior columns.

286
00:23:37,400 --> 00:23:40,820
It was necessary to get the weight onto the outside wall of the building.

287
00:23:41,340 --> 00:23:46,300
A column-free space affords almost any tenant to lay out the floor in the way that is appropriate for their usage.

288
00:23:46,980 --> 00:23:51,260
So it's not just something, I mean, it's something that is commonplace now.

289
00:23:51,261 --> 00:23:57,380
Almost all buildings that we design of any scale now, the goal is to make the floor plates as column-free as possible.

290
00:23:58,180 --> 00:24:01,520
To deal with the problem, Robertson created a unique design.

291
00:24:02,260 --> 00:24:09,180
Instead of beams and columns filling the working spaces of the building, he would create the world's tallest building based on a tube.

292
00:24:10,280 --> 00:24:13,120
A tube is a tremendously strong shape.

293
00:24:13,121 --> 00:24:15,780
It can take large vertical loads.

294
00:24:16,260 --> 00:24:20,620
It can take large lateral forces in the force of wind or earthquakes.

295
00:24:21,260 --> 00:24:26,060
And so it's a tremendous thing, and it puts all the material all the way out on the skin.

296
00:24:26,980 --> 00:24:31,840
Just like if you want to be stable, you spread your legs out and you're much more stable.

297
00:24:32,720 --> 00:24:38,900
The outside of the tube would be a wall of columns tied to an inner core of strong steel shafts.

298
00:24:39,360 --> 00:24:42,900
The solution to getting the outer wall to carry the enormous vertical load

299
00:24:42,901 --> 00:24:45,740
was to keep the vertical columns very closely spaced.

300
00:24:46,500 --> 00:24:50,400
This suited Yamasaki's belief that most people would not be comfortable

301
00:24:50,401 --> 00:24:54,020
standing next to large windows a thousand feet off the ground.

302
00:24:55,240 --> 00:25:00,040
Yamasaki was a very complex guy, brilliant.

303
00:25:00,600 --> 00:25:05,820
He basically relied on other people's judgments.

304
00:25:06,720 --> 00:25:13,220
What he did want was a system where you could put your hands on something solid

305
00:25:13,221 --> 00:25:14,600
on each side of the windows.

306
00:25:14,601 --> 00:25:17,760
that was fundamental to his design.

307
00:25:18,880 --> 00:25:21,560
The forest of columns was now on the outside of the building,

308
00:25:22,020 --> 00:25:22,980
not on the inside.

309
00:25:23,760 --> 00:25:26,540
Even someone afraid of heights could stand next to these windows

310
00:25:26,541 --> 00:25:28,040
and have no fear of falling.

311
00:25:29,500 --> 00:25:31,920
To connect the outer and inner load-bearing columns,

312
00:25:32,300 --> 00:25:34,360
Robertson chose another innovative solution.

313
00:25:35,220 --> 00:25:37,500
Instead of huge I-beams to hold up the floors,

314
00:25:37,860 --> 00:25:40,100
he designed triangular-shaped bar trusses.

315
00:25:40,400 --> 00:25:42,580
These were inexpensive, lightweight,

316
00:25:42,581 --> 00:25:45,580
and more than capable of supporting the weight.

317
00:25:47,580 --> 00:25:50,580
Work began in the summer of 1966.

318
00:25:50,581 --> 00:25:55,580
1.2 million cubic yards of dirt were excavated to make way for the giant buildings.

319
00:25:57,580 --> 00:26:01,580
The 200,000 tons of steel in the Twin Towers went up quickly,

320
00:26:01,581 --> 00:26:03,580
considering the enormity of the task.

321
00:26:04,580 --> 00:26:09,580
The outer columns were put up in modules 10 feet wide, each weighing 22 tons.

322
00:26:09,581 --> 00:26:17,580
The 220 stories of both buildings could house 50,000 workers behind 43,000 windows.

323
00:26:17,581 --> 00:26:23,580
Workers would be cooled by air conditioners that could serve a city of 15,000 homes.

324
00:26:23,581 --> 00:26:30,580
And incredibly, every tenant was provided with workspaces 60 feet wide without a column in sight.

325
00:26:30,581 --> 00:26:36,580
As the last steel was set, Tower 1 topped out at 1,368 feet.

326
00:26:36,581 --> 00:26:39,480
68 feet tower two was six feet shorter

327
00:26:40,240 --> 00:26:47,280
When the World Trade Center towers were at last dedicated in 1973 they were indeed the tallest buildings in the world

328
00:26:48,460 --> 00:26:51,500
It required new approach to

329
00:26:52,720 --> 00:26:56,700
Architecture and engineering not just the structure part but the rest of the building as well

330
00:26:57,080 --> 00:26:59,480
so the remarkable part I think was the fact that

331
00:27:01,100 --> 00:27:03,100
a group of young energetic

332
00:27:03,101 --> 00:27:06,200
folks got together and produced a new kind of building.

333
00:27:09,240 --> 00:27:12,600
Robertson and Yamasaki and all the others could not know it.

334
00:27:13,000 --> 00:27:14,980
But 28 years after their triumph,

335
00:27:15,640 --> 00:27:19,200
their new kind of building would endure a new kind of terror.

336
00:27:22,520 --> 00:27:26,200
Some historians believe the forerunner of the modern skyscraper

337
00:27:26,201 --> 00:27:28,060
was the Ditherington Flax Mill,

338
00:27:28,440 --> 00:27:31,040
built in 1797 in Shrewsbury, England.

339
00:27:31,041 --> 00:27:36,180
It had a cast-iron structural frame, and Viting Disaster will continue on Modern Marvels.

340
00:27:38,280 --> 00:27:45,160
One of the things engineers stay up at night about is the problem of progressive collapse.

341
00:27:46,720 --> 00:27:56,320
The house of cards problem, we might call it, where one piece falls away and takes down other pieces above it and below it.

342
00:27:56,321 --> 00:28:02,060
In the early morning of May 16th, 1968,

343
00:28:02,620 --> 00:28:05,620
a woman awoke in her apartment in the Ronan Point Towers,

344
00:28:06,060 --> 00:28:07,060
east of London, England.

345
00:28:07,760 --> 00:28:10,240
She put a kettle on the stove to make tea.

346
00:28:11,220 --> 00:28:12,280
As she lit a match,

347
00:28:12,780 --> 00:28:15,620
she did not smell the gas that had filled her apartment.

348
00:28:19,120 --> 00:28:23,860
What should have been a very minor gas explosion near a stove

349
00:28:23,861 --> 00:28:31,080
blew out concrete walls and destabilized a whole vertical row of apartments,

350
00:28:31,700 --> 00:28:34,900
causing a collapse all the way down to the ground.

351
00:28:35,580 --> 00:28:41,900
The building had been so poorly designed that the failure of one wall had taken out the floor above,

352
00:28:41,901 --> 00:28:46,400
which collapsed the floor above that until all 22 floors came down.

353
00:28:47,540 --> 00:28:49,680
Amazingly, only four people died.

354
00:28:50,680 --> 00:28:53,240
The future holds a lot of unknowns.

355
00:28:53,860 --> 00:29:07,860
What we want are structures with redundancy, with a factor of safety, to take the major assault, which might be a gas explosion, as in the case of Ronan Point, or it

356
00:29:07,864 --> 00:29:10,240
might be an aircraft impact.

357
00:29:11,400 --> 00:29:16,320
Tuesday, September 11th, 2001, 8.47 a.m.

358
00:29:16,321 --> 00:29:21,900
The Twin Towers of the World Trade Center stand waiting this morning for the men and

359
00:29:21,901 --> 00:29:25,080
women who work in them to begin their day.

360
00:29:25,081 --> 00:29:28,640
Already some 15,000 people are in the buildings.

361
00:29:28,641 --> 00:29:33,700
Some 35,000 will normally be working by midday.

362
00:29:33,701 --> 00:29:38,640
They had lost their status as the tallest buildings in the world years before, but as

363
00:29:38,641 --> 00:29:43,640
they stand now in the morning sun, they remain two of the mightiest structures humans have

364
00:29:43,641 --> 00:29:45,140
ever built.

365
00:29:45,141 --> 00:29:50,080
But even such massive buildings are not designed for what happens next.

366
00:29:50,081 --> 00:29:54,900
Brian Clark had just arrived at his job at a currency trading company on the 84th floor

367
00:29:54,901 --> 00:29:57,500
of the South Tower.

368
00:29:57,501 --> 00:30:04,340
All of a sudden there was this loud boom.

369
00:30:04,341 --> 00:30:09,620
Then there was this sort of glare and I spun around behind me and right outside the windows

370
00:30:09,621 --> 00:30:15,240
on the 84th floor, filling the airspace with swirling, flaming gasoline.

371
00:30:15,440 --> 00:30:17,100
There were flames right there. I didn't know what it was.

372
00:30:18,600 --> 00:30:23,880
What it was, was a Boeing 767 airliner filled with 10,000 gallons of fuel.

373
00:30:24,740 --> 00:30:28,700
The American Airlines plane had slammed into the 96th floor of the North Tower,

374
00:30:29,620 --> 00:30:33,260
killing 92 people on board and setting off a huge explosion.

375
00:30:34,660 --> 00:30:36,620
Stairways in the central column were wrecked,

376
00:30:37,400 --> 00:30:39,700
cutting off escape for those above the impact.

377
00:30:40,500 --> 00:30:42,580
The mighty tower struggles to handle the blow.

378
00:30:43,580 --> 00:30:47,580
100,000 tons of steel is racked by the great force of the plane.

379
00:30:48,440 --> 00:30:51,460
After the airplane hit the building, there was a shockwave that went up and down the tower.

380
00:30:52,020 --> 00:30:56,560
And the building probably moved over on the order of like 18 inches due to this initial impulse.

381
00:30:57,500 --> 00:31:00,480
The tower has sustained massive structural damage.

382
00:31:00,680 --> 00:31:04,100
If the load resting above the impact cannot be held up,

383
00:31:04,101 --> 00:31:10,120
A progressive collapse similar to the Ronan Point apartments will bring the building crashing down.

384
00:31:11,440 --> 00:31:19,480
I think if you had told anyone before 9-11 that you were going to take a building and remove as much of the primary structure from it,

385
00:31:19,940 --> 00:31:27,340
I think you'd be hard-pressed to find an engineer who wouldn't think that the building would be severely impaired and unlikely to survive that kind of event.

386
00:31:28,200 --> 00:31:33,440
Like a prizefighter staggered by a hard blow, the building struggles to stay upright.

387
00:31:34,100 --> 00:31:41,940
The building was fairly robust to be able to take this huge hit and this huge wound and not collapse immediately.

388
00:31:43,060 --> 00:31:48,020
And I have to say, most tall building designers, myself included,

389
00:31:49,100 --> 00:31:53,080
were not overly sure that the building would have survived the initial impact.

390
00:31:54,040 --> 00:31:56,680
But the North Tower continues to stand.

391
00:31:57,180 --> 00:32:00,700
Remarkably, the tremendous weight of the stories above the impact

392
00:32:00,701 --> 00:32:04,040
is redistributed to other remaining beams and columns.

393
00:32:04,800 --> 00:32:07,600
There will be as yet no repeat of one point.

394
00:32:08,600 --> 00:32:10,600
Thousands of people begin to exit the building.

395
00:32:11,600 --> 00:32:18,540
So when the plane went through one face and actually tore out more than half of a face,

396
00:32:18,780 --> 00:32:23,080
instead of the building immediately collapsing, which clearly it could have done,

397
00:32:23,360 --> 00:32:27,060
had it not been for the fact that all these elements were tied together,

398
00:32:27,720 --> 00:32:32,580
that was a wonderful example of redundancy and an alternate load path.

399
00:32:32,581 --> 00:32:39,840
Because of the way in which the World Trade Center was designed, it gave it the unique ability to bridge over this very large kind of gaping wound.

400
00:32:40,260 --> 00:32:43,580
And it allowed it to do that for a significant period of time.

401
00:32:44,680 --> 00:32:49,900
Time that allowed for everyone below the impact zone to be able to exit the building.

402
00:32:51,000 --> 00:32:59,400
As workers in Tower One struggle to get out, those watching the burning building look on in horror as a second plane heads for the South Tower.

403
00:33:02,580 --> 00:33:08,980
United Flight 175, with 65 passengers, rips through the building.

404
00:33:11,180 --> 00:33:14,500
Like its neighbor, the tremendous blow rocks the tower.

405
00:33:16,380 --> 00:33:18,060
And the building moved.

406
00:33:19,020 --> 00:33:23,780
And it went, and it went, and it went, and it seemed like forever.

407
00:33:24,260 --> 00:33:26,720
I'm guessing, but it felt like ten seconds.

408
00:33:26,840 --> 00:33:31,480
It went more, more, and I thought at that moment, we're going over.

409
00:33:31,860 --> 00:33:34,380
Those were the 10 seconds I was terrified that day.

410
00:33:35,600 --> 00:33:41,280
Like Tower 1, the ability of the South Tower to transfer the load keeps the building upright.

411
00:33:41,860 --> 00:33:44,240
But a new threat confronts both towers.

412
00:33:44,900 --> 00:33:49,040
The force of the planes had knocked fireproofing off critical beams and trusses.

413
00:33:49,640 --> 00:33:54,520
Now, thousands of gallons of jet fuel are turning floors into raging infernos.

414
00:33:55,540 --> 00:33:58,380
Each floor of this building is approximately an acre, okay?

415
00:33:58,381 --> 00:34:01,620
So you had four or five acres of building on fire at one time.

416
00:34:02,020 --> 00:34:08,560
You also had all the conventional firefighting systems, such as sprinklers, were undoubtedly taken out by the impact.

417
00:34:09,240 --> 00:34:15,840
The fire starts, it feeds on paperwork in the buildings, becomes so intense, it melts aluminum.

418
00:34:16,420 --> 00:34:25,080
And without the need to actually melt the steel structure, it's enough that those fires, after many minutes, cause the steel to soften.

419
00:34:25,081 --> 00:34:32,580
because redundant support had been planned into them the towers of the world trade center had

420
00:34:32,581 --> 00:34:38,760
withstood violent blows that could have toppled them but fires of such breadth and intensity

421
00:34:38,761 --> 00:34:44,300
had never been imagined by the engineers the building has a series of hat trusses at the top

422
00:34:44,301 --> 00:34:50,040
of the building and these hat trusses like the hat on top of your head linked the core columns

423
00:34:50,041 --> 00:34:52,140
to the perimeter columns.

424
00:34:52,520 --> 00:34:54,740
So as the core columns or the perimeter columns

425
00:34:54,741 --> 00:34:56,340
started to lose strength,

426
00:34:56,420 --> 00:34:58,660
they would shed load to the other columns.

427
00:34:59,060 --> 00:35:01,580
And those columns would take more and more load.

428
00:35:01,660 --> 00:35:04,380
More and more columns would probably fail at some point,

429
00:35:04,381 --> 00:35:06,120
either through loss of support,

430
00:35:06,240 --> 00:35:08,920
because the core trusses might have collapsed if they did,

431
00:35:09,680 --> 00:35:12,760
or just due to fire to the columns themselves.

432
00:35:13,180 --> 00:35:16,080
So the load would go to the neighbor, to the neighbor, to the neighbor.

433
00:35:16,220 --> 00:35:18,740
Eventually, the whole system was overwhelmed

434
00:35:18,741 --> 00:35:19,940
and went down together.

435
00:35:20,040 --> 00:35:24,040
We may never know the exact architecture of failure.

436
00:35:24,041 --> 00:35:28,040
The towers had avoided falling from the plane's impact.

437
00:35:28,041 --> 00:35:35,040
But now, the tremendous heat of the fires brings on the progressive collapse the buildings had so far avoided.

438
00:35:35,041 --> 00:35:41,040
An hour and 25 minutes after it was struck, the south tower comes down.

439
00:35:50,040 --> 00:36:04,040
23 minutes after the collapse of the South Tower, the North Tower also falls.

440
00:36:20,040 --> 00:36:34,040
On the cloudy night of February 20, 1981, an Argentine Airlines Boeing 707 almost struck

441
00:36:35,040 --> 00:36:37,620
the World Trade Center's North Tower.

442
00:36:37,621 --> 00:36:44,040
Inviting disaster will continue on Modern Marvels.

443
00:36:44,041 --> 00:36:53,340
The Trade Center buildings, each a quarter mile high, were very special, irreplaceable.

444
00:36:53,700 --> 00:36:57,660
But we shouldn't forget that there are many other tall buildings in the United States,

445
00:36:58,280 --> 00:37:01,940
469 buildings over 500 feet tall.

446
00:37:02,600 --> 00:37:06,820
In the event of an emergency, any one of those buildings will put the occupants,

447
00:37:06,821 --> 00:37:10,660
particularly those on the top floors, in a very desperate situation.

448
00:37:10,661 --> 00:37:18,000
From now on, people need to know, they need to react, they need to listen.

449
00:37:18,540 --> 00:37:21,580
They just can't wait for someone else's advice.

450
00:37:21,700 --> 00:37:23,980
They've got to know what to do right away.

451
00:37:27,880 --> 00:37:32,260
Over 2,800 people had been buried amidst the twisted pile of rubble

452
00:37:32,261 --> 00:37:35,000
that had once been the mightiest buildings in the world.

453
00:37:35,700 --> 00:37:37,660
The wreckage was still smoldering

454
00:37:37,661 --> 00:37:42,580
when criticism began to be leveled at the engineering that had planned the great buildings.

455
00:37:45,260 --> 00:37:49,220
Some criticized the use of lightweight bar trusses to support the floors.

456
00:37:50,020 --> 00:37:53,320
Others claimed that the fireproofing on the steel beams had been inadequate.

457
00:37:55,900 --> 00:37:59,960
Still other critics suggested that exit stairways were built too close together

458
00:37:59,961 --> 00:38:03,980
and were vulnerable to catastrophic damage in one area of the building.

459
00:38:04,920 --> 00:38:08,240
Two primary inquiries were conducted after the tragedy.

460
00:38:09,260 --> 00:38:12,840
Both came away with several recommendations based on what had happened.

461
00:38:13,380 --> 00:38:15,940
But both put forward similar conclusions.

462
00:38:16,620 --> 00:38:22,460
Given the massive trauma the World Trade Center had received, the buildings had actually done very well.

463
00:38:22,461 --> 00:38:28,120
The fact is, no designer in the late 1960s

464
00:38:28,121 --> 00:38:30,360
could have properly anticipated the forces

465
00:38:30,361 --> 00:38:34,040
that were brought to bear against the towers over 30 years later.

466
00:38:34,960 --> 00:38:39,320
It was never assumed by Leslie Robertson and his associates

467
00:38:39,321 --> 00:38:43,620
that the collision would be from an intentional act,

468
00:38:44,520 --> 00:38:48,020
namely the biggest airplane the terrorists could get their hands on

469
00:38:48,021 --> 00:38:50,620
and that it would be filled with jet fuel.

470
00:38:51,420 --> 00:38:58,620
The Boeing 767 flown by the terrorists was much larger than the 707 airliner planned for in the 1960s.

471
00:38:59,280 --> 00:39:04,740
The design team also anticipated a plane hitting the building by accident and not design.

472
00:39:05,480 --> 00:39:07,840
The big difference is the amount of fuel.

473
00:39:07,980 --> 00:39:16,260
The 707 we envisioned was one lost in the fog, throttles back, trying to find JFK instead of the throttle down.

474
00:39:16,261 --> 00:39:24,860
So the energy of impact of the aircraft was very much larger than was the design criteria.

475
00:39:28,040 --> 00:39:34,560
The painful reality of what happened on 9-11 has not lessened interest in tall buildings.

476
00:39:35,120 --> 00:39:41,500
From Hong Kong to Barcelona, over 4,000 buildings of over 12 stories are currently being built.

477
00:39:42,460 --> 00:39:46,400
Each will present problems of protection and safety to the people who use them.

478
00:39:47,340 --> 00:39:51,400
Each will need to study the lessons that must be learned from the World Trade Center.

479
00:39:52,500 --> 00:39:55,820
Perhaps the most critical area to be looked into is evacuation.

480
00:39:56,900 --> 00:40:01,500
The stairways in the Twin Towers allowed many people to escape from a very dangerous situation.

481
00:40:02,440 --> 00:40:05,840
But the World Trade Center was barely half full on that point.

482
00:40:05,841 --> 00:40:12,180
If the attack had happened a few hours later, another 20,000 people would have been trying to get out.

483
00:40:13,100 --> 00:40:17,380
To evacuate fully occupied buildings could have taken well over three hours.

484
00:40:18,060 --> 00:40:19,280
Too long in this case.

485
00:40:20,080 --> 00:40:24,500
Builders must change their attitude about what can be expected of our escape systems.

486
00:40:25,460 --> 00:40:28,440
Architects and engineers, when they're considering tall buildings,

487
00:40:28,540 --> 00:40:31,760
will have to re-examine the assumptions they've made in the past.

488
00:40:31,761 --> 00:40:38,520
The most basic assumption they've made in the past is that you don't have to evacuate a high-rise building totally.

489
00:40:39,480 --> 00:40:41,440
We now know that that's a bad assumption.

490
00:40:42,560 --> 00:40:47,780
Planners also need to rethink how to get firemen to the source of the fire in tall buildings.

491
00:40:48,800 --> 00:40:58,460
One of the hopes that we carry with us is that firefighters will always be able to come and get us out of danger.

492
00:40:58,461 --> 00:41:09,220
But high floors in a skyscraper are a very remote place when it comes to firefighters getting up to help you.

493
00:41:10,560 --> 00:41:14,080
Another lesson may be found in the way the stairways are designed.

494
00:41:14,860 --> 00:41:20,860
Although thousands of people in the Twin Towers owe their lives to those stairs, major concerns presented themselves.

495
00:41:22,400 --> 00:41:27,360
As in most tall buildings, the stairs in the World Trade Center had been grouped around the central core.

496
00:41:28,260 --> 00:41:33,900
Using this design, stairs would receive the structural protection of the steel surrounding the elevator shafts.

497
00:41:34,540 --> 00:41:39,720
But when the terrorist-guided planes burst through the towers, most stairways were destroyed.

498
00:41:40,240 --> 00:41:42,740
Future designs may change stair placement.

499
00:41:45,040 --> 00:41:50,620
There are many lessons from the World Trade Center which will impact how future tall buildings are designed.

500
00:41:50,621 --> 00:41:53,860
But one thing seems certain to engineers and architects.

501
00:41:53,861 --> 00:42:00,860
We cannot create designs that anticipate a particular terrorist-guided aircraft.

502
00:42:00,861 --> 00:42:06,860
We do not design buildings for the impact of planes then or even now.

503
00:42:06,861 --> 00:42:09,860
There will be bigger and faster planes.

504
00:42:09,861 --> 00:42:15,860
And if we were to design for it, the building will be uninhabitable.

505
00:42:15,861 --> 00:42:20,860
One wouldn't want to live or work in a building like that. It would be a bunker.

506
00:42:20,861 --> 00:42:23,960
Tall buildings will continue to be built.

507
00:42:24,520 --> 00:42:30,460
These amazing technical accomplishments represent a practical solution to problems of how people live and work.

508
00:42:31,700 --> 00:42:38,040
In spite of the events of September 11th, it is important that we remember that in an ongoing world of skyscrapers,

509
00:42:38,220 --> 00:42:40,760
these amazing structures remain very safe.

510
00:42:42,260 --> 00:42:44,040
Tall buildings are very, very safe.

511
00:42:44,041 --> 00:42:46,800
I mean, I live and work in a very tall building.

512
00:42:46,801 --> 00:42:51,420
And I would tell everybody that they're the safest buildings around.

513
00:42:52,620 --> 00:42:58,660
From a structural point of view, as far as I'm concerned, high-rise buildings are perfectly safe.

514
00:42:59,140 --> 00:43:04,480
There's no reason for them to present a danger to their occupants in any way.

515
00:43:04,860 --> 00:43:07,060
So, yes, they are safe.

516
00:43:07,061 --> 00:43:17,000
But those who choose to hate used two symbols of America to send their terrible message.

517
00:43:17,660 --> 00:43:21,600
The World Trade Center towers fought and lost a fight to stay erect.

518
00:43:22,520 --> 00:43:25,600
And innocent people were the victims of hatred and terror.

519
00:43:27,000 --> 00:43:28,780
People ask me how I felt about it.

520
00:43:28,940 --> 00:43:34,200
I have to tell you, I spent a huge amount of my lifetime working on the design of the World Trade Center.

521
00:43:34,201 --> 00:43:37,240
It was an extremely important project to me personally.

522
00:43:38,480 --> 00:43:39,380
But it's a building.

523
00:43:40,560 --> 00:43:44,180
The loss of the building was important,

524
00:43:44,181 --> 00:43:49,120
but the only thing that was on my mind then and now

525
00:43:49,121 --> 00:43:51,300
is the fact that people lost their lives.

526
00:43:52,060 --> 00:43:54,560
Buildings can be replaced, hopefully, with better ones.

527
00:43:55,220 --> 00:44:00,080
But there's no way to bring back people who are lost.

528
00:44:00,081 --> 00:44:06,780
We will learn from the experience of September 11th

529
00:44:06,781 --> 00:44:09,380
Buildings will become safer and more secure

530
00:44:09,381 --> 00:44:13,060
As architects and engineers work to plan for the unexpected

531
00:44:13,061 --> 00:44:17,320
But along the way, we will never forget those who perished

532
00:44:17,321 --> 00:44:22,460
As two magnificent buildings struggled in vain to avoid disaster

